In perturbative string theory, a bosonic string is described by a two-dimensional worldsheet sigma model with bosonic coordinate fields X^μ(σ,τ) embedding the worldsheet into a D-dimensional target spacetime. To incorporate spacetime fermions, one extends this sigma model by introducing worldsheet superpartners ψ^μ(σ,τ), producing a (1,0) or (1,1) supersymmetric worldsheet theory (the Ramond–Neveu–Schwarz formulation); alternatively, one may impose spacetime supersymmetry directly (the Green–Schwarz formulation). Quantum consistency of the worldsheet superconformal field theory fixes the critical target spacetime dimension (D=10 for the superstring, D=26 for the bosonic string). At one loop, generic non-supersymmetric string vacua exhibit a non-vanishing dilaton tadpole that destabilizes the vacuum; spacetime supersymmetry guarantees the cancellation of this tadpole. In four-dimensional heterotic compactifications with an anomalous U(1) gauge symmetry whose mixed anomalies are removed by the Green–Schwarz mechanism, a one-loop Fayet–Iliopoulos D-term proportional to Tr Q_X is generated; vacuum expectation values for charged scalars must shift to cancel this D-term, restoring supersymmetry and possibly generating intermediate mass scales.

Articulate, with mathematical precision, the following:
(a) the mechanism by which worldsheet supersymmetry of the (N=1 supersymmetric) sigma model gives rise to spacetime fermions in the target theory, and the precise conditions (GSO projection, modular invariance) under which worldsheet supersymmetry implies spacetime supersymmetry;
(b) the derivation of the critical spacetime dimension from cancellation of the conformal/superconformal anomaly, in both the bosonic and supersymmetric cases;
(c) the statement and proof that unbroken spacetime supersymmetry forces the one-loop dilaton tadpole (vacuum energy) to vanish, and the converse question of whether vanishing tadpoles imply supersymmetry;
(d) the computation of the one-loop Fayet–Iliopoulos parameter ξ ∝ Tr Q_X in heterotic compactifications with a pseudo-anomalous U(1), and the description of the resulting vacuum-shifting flat directions that restore supersymmetry while breaking the anomalous U(1) and possibly generating intermediate mass scales.

State to what extent each of (a)–(d) is mathematically established in perturbative string theory.

